@node Capture
@section Capture

@findex slack-extras-personal-capture
The command @code{slack-extras-personal-capture} captures the Slack message at point to the personal inbox via @code{org-capture} template @samp{s}. It is bound to @samp{o} in @code{slack-activity-feed-buffer-mode-map}@comma{} @code{slack-thread-message-buffer-mode-map} and @code{slack-message-buffer-mode-map}.

@findex slack-extras-work-capture
The command @code{slack-extras-work-capture} captures the Slack message at point to the work inbox via @code{org-capture} template @samp{S}. It is bound to @samp{E} in @code{slack-activity-feed-buffer-mode-map}@comma{} @code{slack-thread-message-buffer-mode-map} and @code{slack-message-buffer-mode-map}.

@node ActivityWatch context
@chapter ActivityWatch context

@findex slack-extras-activity-watch-data
When @samp{activity-watch-mode} is loaded@comma{} @code{slack-extras-activity-watch-data} contributes channel and workspace names and IDs through @code{activity-watch-data-functions}. It reads the room object already held by the sampled Slack buffer. This lets a time tracker use the selected channel rather than the buffer's unrelated inherited working directory.

Public and private channels@comma{} including their threads@comma{} provide channel context. Direct messages@comma{} group direct messages and mixed-room views provide only workspace context@comma{} allowing work conversations to remain general work without inventing a project. The integration reads no messages and makes no network requests. ActivityWatch receives the metadata only when its watcher samples the selected buffer of a focused frame.

@node Tab-bar notifications
@chapter Tab-bar notifications

@vindex tab-bar-extras-slack-notifications-enabled
The variable @code{tab-bar-extras-slack-notifications-enabled} controls whether the Slack notification element is displayed in the tab bar. It defaults to @code{t}.

@findex slack-extras-actionable-unreads-p
The function @code{slack-extras-actionable-unreads-p} determines whether there are Slack notifications worth the user's attention. It triggers a debounced refresh of the Activity feed unread summary and returns the value of @code{slack-has-unreads}.

The tab-bar element @code{tab-bar-extras-slack-element} displays a Slack icon (from @samp{nerd-icons}) and a badge when @code{slack-extras-actionable-unreads-p} returns non-nil. The badge shows the total mention count@comma{} or a bullet (@code{•}) when there are actionable unreads but no @@mentions (such as thread replies or DMs). Registration appends the element to @code{tab-bar-extras-global-mode-string} and immediately syncs @code{global-mode-string} via @code{tab-bar-extras-set-global-mode-string}@comma{} so the badge appears even when @samp{slack-extras} loads after the initial tab-bar sync.

@findex tab-bar-extras-toggle-slack-notifications
The command @code{tab-bar-extras-toggle-slack-notifications} toggles Slack notifications in the tab bar. It also participates in the master toggle: when @code{tab-bar-extras-toggle-notifications} is called@comma{} Slack notifications are toggled along with the other notification sources.

@node Credentials from Chrome
@chapter Credentials from Chrome

@samp{emacs-slack} authenticates with a browser session: an @samp{xoxc-} token and the @samp{d} cookie. Slack invalidates both whenever that browser session ends@comma{} so credentials copied once into 1Password go stale. This section registers teams with the credentials Chrome currently holds instead.

@vindex slack-extras-teams
The variable @code{slack-extras-teams} lists the teams to register. Each element is a plist with @samp{:domain} (the workspace host@comma{} such as @samp{"epochai.slack.com"})@comma{} @samp{:fallback} (a function of no arguments returning @samp{(TOKEN . COOKIE)}@comma{} typically read from 1Password)@comma{} and any other @code{slack-register-team} options@comma{} such as @samp{:name} and @samp{:animate-image}.

@findex slack-extras-register-teams
The command @code{slack-extras-register-teams} registers every team in @code{slack-extras-teams}. For each team it asks @code{slack-extras-chrome-credentials} for the current pair and uses @samp{:fallback} only when that returns nil. Every team already registered under the same name with a different token is disconnected@comma{} its buffers are killed and it is removed from the team registries first@comma{} so calling the command again after a sign-out refreshes the team without leaving a duplicate. The buffers must go@comma{} and @code{slack-current-team} moves to the new team@comma{} because both hold the old team object and the Activity feed would otherwise register it again. A team that was connected is connected again under the new token.

@findex slack-extras-chrome-credentials
@vindex slack-extras-chrome-program
@vindex slack-extras-chrome-cache-directory
The function @code{slack-extras-chrome-credentials} runs @code{slack-extras-chrome-program} (@samp{claude/bin/slack_chrome.py ensure DOMAIN})@comma{} which verifies the cached pair with @samp{auth.test} and@comma{} if Slack rejects it@comma{} reads the @samp{d} cookie from Chrome's cookie database and derives the token from the workspace's @samp{/ssb/redirect} page. It then reads the pair from @samp{DOMAIN.json} in @code{slack-extras-chrome-cache-directory}. It returns nil when the program fails@comma{} for example when no Chrome profile is signed in to that workspace.

@node Reachability before connecting
@chapter Reachability before connecting

@findex slack-extras--open-when-reachable
On macOS@comma{} opening a websocket resolves its host on Emacs's main thread@comma{} so a reconnect while the resolver is slow freezes Emacs. @samp{slack-extras} therefore advises @code{slack-ws-open} to check first@comma{} from a subprocess with @code{simple-extras-probe-url} (see the @samp{simple-extras} manual)@comma{} whether the websocket host answers. Only the host is probed@comma{} never the URL's token. If the host answers@comma{} the websocket opens as before. If not@comma{} the open is skipped and @code{slack-ws-reconnect} schedules another attempt@comma{} with its usual backoff and attempt limit.

Only one probe per team runs at a time; an open requested meanwhile is dropped. When the probe ends@comma{} the open is also dropped@comma{} with no reconnect@comma{} if the team was unregistered or replaced@comma{} reconnection was turned off@comma{} the websocket got connected@comma{} or another connection attempt was made in the meantime.

@node Completing to-dos
@chapter Completing to-dos

A message marked with Slack's ``Add to to-do'' becomes a task through Protean@comma{} whose task carries the properties @samp{PROTEAN_SLACK_WORKSPACE}@comma{} @samp{PROTEAN_SLACK_CHANNEL}@comma{} @samp{PROTEAN_SLACK_TS} and @samp{PROTEAN_SLACK_MARK} (the mark's creation time@comma{} which tells a later re-mark of the same message apart). This section closes the loop: when the task is closed in Org@comma{} the to-do is completed in Slack. Protean's engine never writes to Slack@comma{} so the write happens here.

@findex slack-extras-protean-todo-closed
The function @code{slack-extras-protean-todo-closed} belongs on @code{protean-user-closed-functions}@comma{} which runs only for a closure the user made and saved@comma{} never for one Protean's own code made. For a task with all four properties it adds the to-do to a queue and starts completing it; any other task is ignored.

@findex slack-extras-todo-flush
@vindex slack-extras-todo-queue-file
@vindex slack-extras-slack-program
@vindex slack-extras-todo-retry-seconds
@vindex slack-extras-todo-timeout-seconds
The queue lives in @code{slack-extras-todo-queue-file}@comma{} so it survives a restart. @code{slack-extras-todo-flush} runs @code{slack-extras-slack-program} (@samp{claude/bin/slack.py -w WORKSPACE complete-todo CHANNEL TS --mark=MARK}) asynchronously for each queued to-do. That command lists every page of the workspace's saved items and completes the to-do@comma{} as Slack's own client does@comma{} only if the message is still a to-do with the same mark; a to-do already completed or removed@comma{} turned into a plain saved item@comma{} or marked again is left alone. Either way the entry leaves the queue. A failure@comma{} or an attempt still running after @code{slack-extras-todo-timeout-seconds}@comma{} leaves it queued@comma{} says so in the echo area@comma{} and a timer tries again every @code{slack-extras-todo-retry-seconds} while the queue holds entries. Call @code{slack-extras-todo-flush} after startup to resume a queue left by an earlier session.

